00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:19.040
Speaker 5: I was actually thinking about a
interview I heard with Ezra Klein a while

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.170
back, talking about if we are entering an
era of post persuasion, politics, meaning

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.770
that so many people seem to be subject to
asymmetrical polarization or a stronger level

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.110
of polarization.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.009
And I wanted to note in your observation,
because you've been in the game for a long

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:45.219
time, do you see the era of politics that
we are in now in terms of retail politics,

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:47.100
electoral politics?

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.840
Do you see us moving or are we already in
an area where it's all about registering,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.530
cultivating and turning out a base?

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:00.450
Or do you see that there is still room for
persuasion on a broader level?

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.310
Speaker 1: I think it's mostly the former
and there is some of the latter.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.610
I mean, I think you're starting to get these
smaller and smaller you.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.081
You know, one of the genius things that Trump
did and I'm not even saying that he did it

00:02:13.081 --> 00:02:19.200
on purpose, it just happened, is that he reached
people who had not been paying attention to

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.100
politics in any real way.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.830
So they didn't even realize that they were
being lied to, that Trump's campaign promises

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.980
were impossible, that they were being propagandized
and on and on and on.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.160
So what was genius about it was they completely
uncritically were just like, Oh, Trump, Oh

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.550
yeah, I'm voting for Trump.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.800
And they were insulated from any kind of critical
thought.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.250
And that's why when the Access Hollywood tape
came out, it didn't make a difference in all

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.060
these different things.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:52.320
Increasingly, to win, you have to find those
groups of people that have not yet become

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.480
asymmetrically rattled, radicalize the way
that you're pointing out, or just asymmetrically

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.150
sort of devoted to one side or the other.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.200
And Trump, Trump was able to do that.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.200
So I think that it's a and it's an important
observation and it's an accurate one.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.250
Speaker 5: And just as a quick follow up to
that, do you think that the current infrastructure

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:17.010
and the current messaging that the broader
Democratic Party and candidates have is capable

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:22.680
of fulfilling the the mission there, I guess,
that you outlined in terms of being able to

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.870
find folks that are able to be persuaded and
create a media ecosystem that promotes that

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:29.870
has.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:30.870
Speaker 1: I mean, not so far.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.500
So far, they've failed on two fronts.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.500
One, they've failed to properly fund the effort
to create that ecosystem.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.180
And number two, they failed to identify a
contingent of the half of the country that

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.820
doesn't vote, that could be sort of assembled
as a voting bloc and activated.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:52.110
So so far, they've not proven they can do
it.

